T.append(("B01","Botany","""Here's an experiment that sounds almost too simple. In the late 1990s, an ecologist working in the western United States clipped the leaves of sagebrush plants with scissors, imitating a grazing insect. Wild tobacco plants growing next to the clipped sagebrush then suffered less damage from leaf-eating insects over the season than tobacco growing next to untouched sagebrush. Nothing touched the tobacco. So what happened?

The best explanation is airborne chemistry. Damaged sagebrush releases a burst of volatile compounds, and nearby plants seem to respond by ramping up their own defenses. Later work found that sagebrush responds to cues from other sagebrush, too, and, interestingly, it seems to respond more strongly when the damaged neighbor is a close relative.

Now, the popular version of this is that plants talk to each other, that they warn their neighbors. I'd be careful with that word. Think about it from the damaged plant's point of view. Why would it pay to alert a competitor growing a meter away, one that's fighting it for water and light? Usually it wouldn't. The tobacco plant that picks up the signal benefits, sure, but that looks less like a warning and more like eavesdropping.

So why release the chemicals at all? One idea some researchers favor is that the main audience is the plant itself. In sagebrush, the internal plumbing that carries water and nutrients is divided into sectors, so a chemical message traveling inside the plant may never reach a branch on the far side. Air, though, reaches everything. On that view, the plant is signaling to its own distant branches, and the neighbors are just overhearing.

That doesn't settle it, and the result with relatives still needs explaining. But whenever you hear the word communication, ask who actually benefits.""",[
 Q("What is the main purpose of the talk?","medium",["To question whether plants really warn one another","To show that tobacco defends itself better than sagebrush","To explain how insects find damaged sagebrush plants","To argue that plants compete mainly through the air"],"To question whether plants really warn one another","The professor says 'I'd be careful with that word' about plants warning neighbors and offers eavesdropping and self-signaling as alternatives.",["The two species' defenses are never ranked.","How insects find plants is not discussed.","Competition is mentioned for water and light, not air."]),
 Q("According to the professor, what happened to the tobacco next to the clipped sagebrush?","medium",["Insects damaged it less over the season","It was clipped too, to imitate grazing","It grew more slowly than other tobacco","It drew more insects than other tobacco"],"Insects damaged it less over the season","Tobacco next to clipped sagebrush 'suffered less damage from leaf-eating insects over the season'.",["'Nothing touched the tobacco.'","Its growth rate is not compared.","It suffered less insect damage, the opposite."]),
 Q("Why does the professor mention that the plumbing inside sagebrush is divided into sectors?","hard",["To suggest why a plant might signal its far branches by air","To show why sagebrush cannot respond to signals from its neighbors","To explain why clipped branches of sagebrush recover so slowly","To show that sagebrush and wild tobacco are closely related"],"To suggest why a plant might signal its far branches by air","An internal message 'may never reach a branch on the far side. Air, though, reaches everything.'",["Sagebrush 'responds to cues from other sagebrush'.","Recovery of clipped branches is not discussed.","Relatedness is discussed among sagebrush plants only."]),
 Q("What is the professor's view of the claim that plants warn their neighbors?","hard",["Doubtful, since warning a rival usually gains it nothing","Convinced, since the tobacco experiment demonstrates it","Undecided, since no experiment has yet tested the idea","Doubtful, since neighbors do not respond to the chemicals"],"Doubtful, since warning a rival usually gains it nothing","'Why would it pay to alert a competitor...? Usually it wouldn't.'",["He reads the tobacco result as eavesdropping, not warning.","He describes experiments and states a clear doubt.","Nearby plants 'seem to respond by ramping up their own defenses'."])]))
T.append(("B02","Linguistics","""On La Gomera, one of the Canary Islands, there's a way of speaking Spanish without using your voice at all. It's called Silbo Gomero, and it's whistled. The island is cut by deep ravines, and a whistle can carry across a valley, up to a few kilometers in good conditions, where a shout would die out. And it's not a code with a fixed list of messages. Skilled whistlers can say more or less anything they could say in spoken Spanish.

So how do you whistle a language? A whistle has basically one dimension to play with: pitch. Whistlers map the sounds of speech onto changes in pitch. Vowels become different pitch levels, and consonants show up in the way the pitch rises, falls, or breaks off between them. A lot of information gets lost, obviously, and listeners lean heavily on context.

Here's where it gets interesting for the brain. About twenty years ago, researchers scanned experienced Silbo whistlers and found that whistled Spanish activated the same left-hemisphere regions that handle spoken language. In people who didn't know Silbo, those regions didn't respond that way. That was taken as nice evidence that the brain's language areas care about language, not about the kind of sound it arrives in.

But a later study, on a whistled form of Turkish used in a village in northeastern Turkey, complicated the picture. With a listening test that sends different sounds to each ear, researchers found that whistled Turkish drew on both hemispheres far more evenly than spoken Turkish did. Now, the right hemisphere is generally better at handling melody and pitch, and whistling is all pitch.

So which is it? Probably both. The left hemisphere's lead in language looks real, but it isn't absolute. How a language reaches the ear seems to change how the brain divides the work.""",[
 Q("According to the professor, why is whistling useful on La Gomera?","medium",["A whistle crosses the deep ravines where a shout would fade","Whistling lets islanders talk without knowing Spanish","A whistle can carry more information than ordinary speech","Islanders use it for a short, fixed list of warning signals"],"A whistle crosses the deep ravines where a shout would fade","'A whistle can carry across a valley... where a shout would die out.'",["It is whistled Spanish; whistlers say what they 'could say in spoken Spanish'.","'A lot of information gets lost.'","'It's not a code with a fixed list of messages.'"]),
 Q("How are consonants represented in Silbo Gomero?","medium",["By how the pitch rises, falls, or breaks off","By steady pitch levels held for a moment","By changes in how loudly the whistle sounds","By short silences placed between whole words"],"By how the pitch rises, falls, or breaks off","Consonants 'show up in the way the pitch rises, falls, or breaks off between them'.",["Steady pitch levels are how vowels are represented.","A whistle has 'basically one dimension to play with: pitch'.","Pitch breaks fall between sounds, not words."]),
 Q("Why does the professor mention that the right hemisphere is better at handling melody and pitch?","hard",["To suggest why whistled Turkish used the right hemisphere more","To explain why people who didn't know Silbo could not follow it","To show that spoken Turkish depends mainly on melody and pitch","To argue that the earlier study of Silbo whistlers was flawed"],"To suggest why whistled Turkish used the right hemisphere more","Right after the Turkish result: the right hemisphere handles pitch, 'and whistling is all pitch'.",["The remark follows the Turkish study, not the non-whistlers.","Spoken Turkish was the comparison with less even use.","He says 'Probably both' are right."]),
 Q("What does the professor conclude about the left hemisphere's role in language?","hard",["It is real, but how a language sounds can shift it","It was disproved by the study of whistled Turkish","It applies only to languages that have a written form","It is stronger for whistled than for spoken languages"],"It is real, but how a language sounds can shift it","'The left hemisphere's lead in language looks real, but it isn't absolute.'",["He calls the lead 'real'.","Writing is never discussed.","Whistled Turkish used the left hemisphere less, not more."])]))
T.append(("B03","Urban Planning","""When a highway gets congested, the obvious fix is to add lanes. More room, less traffic, right? It's intuitive, and it mostly doesn't work, at least not for long.

Economists have looked at this carefully. One well-known American study compared metropolitan areas over a couple of decades and found that the total amount of driving grew roughly in proportion to the amount of new road built. Add ten percent more lane capacity, and you get something close to ten percent more driving. So where do the new drivers come from? Some people who used to take the bus switch to driving. Some who traveled at off-peak hours move back into rush hour. Businesses and new housing locate near the faster road. The space fills up. Planners call this induced demand.

Now, if adding roads creates traffic, does removing roads make traffic disappear? Seoul offers a famous test. In the early 2000s, the city tore down an elevated highway that ran over the Cheonggyecheon stream, which had been paved over decades earlier, and restored the stream as a public park. Plenty of people predicted gridlock. It didn't happen. Traffic in the surrounding area didn't get dramatically worse, and some drivers simply stopped making those trips by car.

But be careful about the lesson. Seoul didn't just remove a road. Around the same time, the city reorganized its whole bus system, with dedicated bus lanes and a new fare system, and it already had an extensive subway. The drivers who vanished had somewhere to go. Take away a highway in a city where the car is the only realistic option, and you may simply push the traffic onto neighboring streets.

So traffic responds to road space in both directions. It just doesn't respond in a vacuum.""",[
 Q("What is the main idea of the talk?","medium",["Traffic follows road space, but alternatives shape the result","Cities should remove highways in order to reduce congestion","Seoul's bus reform made downtown traffic noticeably heavier","Adding lanes reduces congestion if it is done gradually"],"Traffic follows road space, but alternatives shape the result","'Traffic responds to road space in both directions. It just doesn't respond in a vacuum.'",["He warns that removal elsewhere may 'push the traffic onto neighboring streets'.","Traffic 'didn't get dramatically worse'.","Added capacity fills up; no gradual method is mentioned."]),
 Q("According to the professor, who are some of the new drivers on a widened road?","medium",["People who used to travel outside rush hour","Commuters who move away from the new road","Drivers pushed off streets that were closed","People who could no longer afford the bus"],"People who used to travel outside rush hour","'Some who traveled at off-peak hours move back into rush hour.'",["Housing locates 'near the faster road', not away from it.","Closed streets belong to the road-removal case, not widening.","Bus riders switch, but no cost reason is given."]),
 Q("Why does the professor mention Seoul's reorganized bus system?","hard",["To show that drivers who left had other ways to get around","To explain why the city decided to remove the highway at all","To show that the new bus lanes caused the predicted gridlock","To argue that buses are more popular in Seoul than the subway"],"To show that drivers who left had other ways to get around","'The drivers who vanished had somewhere to go.'",["The reasons for the removal are not given.","Gridlock 'didn't happen'.","Buses and subway are not ranked."]),
 Q("What would the professor most likely predict if a highway were removed in a city with little public transit?","hard",["Traffic would probably move onto nearby streets","Most drivers would stop making the trips by car","Driving would fall by about ten percent overall","The city would soon have to build a new subway"],"Traffic would probably move onto nearby streets","Without alternatives, 'you may simply push the traffic onto neighboring streets.'",["That happened in Seoul, where drivers had alternatives.","The ten percent figure concerns adding lanes, not removal.","He makes no claim about building transit afterwards."])]))
T.append(("B04","Economic History","""You've probably heard of tulip mania. In the Dutch Republic in the 1630s, the story goes, people went mad for tulip bulbs. Single bulbs sold for the price of a house, ordinary people mortgaged everything, and when prices collapsed in early 1637, the country was ruined. Some versions add merchants throwing themselves into canals. It's the classic tale of a bubble, and much of it was popularized two centuries later, in 1841, by a Scottish writer named Charles Mackay.

The trouble is, when historians went back to the archives, the ruin is hard to find. One historian, Anne Goldgar, worked through contracts, court records, and notaries' papers from the period, and she found very few bankruptcies that could be tied to tulips. The trade seems to have been concentrated among fairly well-off merchants and craftsmen, not the poor. And the Dutch economy appears to have kept growing through the late 1630s. So where did the stories of mass ruin come from? Largely from moralizing pamphlets printed right after the crash, which used tulips to preach against greed. Mackay leaned on material like that.

Now, I don't want to swing too far the other way. Prices really did spike, and they really did collapse. That part is well documented. Some rare varieties changed hands for astonishing sums. And a lot of the trading in the final months was in contracts for bulbs still in the ground, so when buyers refused to pay, there were real disputes, and some of them dragged on for a while.

What the evidence undermines is the idea that the crash was an economic disaster. A price bubble and a financial catastrophe aren't the same thing. One can happen without the other, and here, it seems, it mostly did.""",[
 Q("What is the main point of the talk?","medium",["The crash was real but did not bring the ruin often claimed","Tulip prices never actually rose or fell very sharply","Poor workers lost the most money when tulip prices finally fell","Mackay based his account mainly on Dutch court records"],"The crash was real but did not bring the ruin often claimed","'What the evidence undermines is the idea that the crash was an economic disaster.' Yet 'Prices really did spike, and they really did collapse.'",["'Prices really did spike, and they really did collapse.'","The trade was concentrated among the well-off, 'not the poor'.","Court records were Goldgar's source; Mackay leaned on pamphlets."]),
 Q("According to the professor, where did the stories of mass ruin mostly come from?","medium",["Pamphlets printed after the crash to preach against greed","Bankruptcy records kept by Dutch courts during the 1630s","Letters written by merchants who had lost their fortunes","Contracts showing what the poor had paid for single bulbs"],"Pamphlets printed after the crash to preach against greed","'Largely from moralizing pamphlets printed right after the crash, which used tulips to preach against greed.'",["Court records showed 'very few bankruptcies' tied to tulips.","No such letters are cited.","The poor were not the main traders, and contracts did not show ruin."]),
 Q("Why does the professor mention contracts for bulbs that were still in the ground?","hard",["To grant that the collapse did cause some real trouble","To show that the price spike was invented by Mackay","To explain how poor families were able to join the trade","To argue that trading in tulips was against Dutch law"],"To grant that the collapse did cause some real trouble","In the 'I don't want to swing too far' section: when buyers refused to pay, 'there were real disputes'.",["The spike is 'well documented'.","The trade was concentrated among the well-off.","Legality is never discussed."]),
 Q("What does the professor imply by saying that a price bubble and a financial catastrophe 'aren't the same thing'?","hard",["Prices can collapse without wrecking the wider economy","A catastrophe always follows a bubble within a few years","The tulip price rise was too small to count as a bubble","Historians should no longer call tulip mania a bubble"],"Prices can collapse without wrecking the wider economy","'One can happen without the other, and here, it seems, it mostly did' - prices crashed while the economy kept growing.",["The point is that one can occur without the other.","Rare bulbs sold 'for astonishing sums'; the spike was real.","He accepts the price bubble as real and documented."])]))
T.append(("B05","Neuroscience","""For most of the twentieth century, the textbook rule was that you're born with all the neurons you'll ever have. Then, starting in the 1960s and especially in the 1990s, studies in rats and mice showed that new neurons keep being born in adult brains, in one region in particular: the hippocampus, which is crucial for memory. The obvious next question is whether the same is true for us. And here, the field is still arguing.

Some of the evidence is clever. One team took advantage of the nuclear weapons tests of the 1950s and 60s, which raised the level of carbon-14 in the atmosphere. That carbon ended up in the DNA of cells born during those years, so by measuring it in brains donated after death, researchers could estimate when the neurons had been born. Their estimate was that adults add something like seven hundred new neurons a day to the hippocampus.

But in 2018, one group looked directly at hippocampal tissue, staining for markers that young neurons carry, and found essentially none in adults. In babies, yes. In young teenagers, a few. In adults, almost nothing. Another lab published the opposite result that same year.

How can two careful labs disagree that much? One possibility is the tissue itself. Human brain samples are collected hours after death and preserved in different ways, and some of the markers for young neurons seem to fade quickly if the tissue isn't preserved right away. A follow-up study that controlled preservation tightly did find young neurons, even in people in their late eighties.

So here's where I land. The case for adult neurogenesis in humans looks stronger now than it did in 2018, but the debate isn't over, and what's being argued about is partly the methods, not just the brain.""",[
 Q("Why were researchers able to use carbon-14 to date neurons?","hard",["Weapons tests raised atmospheric carbon-14, marking cells born then","Neurons produce carbon-14 at a steady rate as they grow older and die","Carbon-14 binds to the markers that young neurons carry","Carbon-14 levels in the air have stayed the same since 1950"],"Weapons tests raised atmospheric carbon-14, marking cells born then","The tests 'raised the level of carbon-14 in the atmosphere. That carbon ended up in the DNA of cells born during those years.'",["The carbon comes from the atmosphere, not the neurons.","Markers were stained in the separate 2018 study.","The tests raised the level, so it did not stay the same."]),
 Q("What did the 2018 study that stained for young-neuron markers find?","medium",["Markers in babies but almost none in adults","About seven hundred new neurons a day","Young neurons in people in their eighties","No young neurons at any age, even in babies"],"Markers in babies but almost none in adults","'In babies, yes. In young teenagers, a few. In adults, almost nothing.'",["That figure came from the carbon-14 study.","That was the later follow-up study.","Babies did show them."]),
 Q("Why does the professor discuss how brain tissue is preserved?","medium",["To suggest a reason the labs' results conflicted","To explain why most studies use rats and mice instead","To show that the carbon-14 estimate was wrong","To argue that brain donation should be limited"],"To suggest a reason the labs' results conflicted","'How can two careful labs disagree that much? One possibility is the tissue itself.'",["Rodent studies are mentioned only as background.","The carbon-14 estimate is not questioned.","No such argument is made."]),
 Q("What is the professor's position on adult neurogenesis in humans?","hard",["The evidence has grown stronger, but the question is still open","Adults produce no new neurons, as the 2018 staining study showed","The 2018 staining study was carelessly done and can be ignored","The follow-up study has settled the question once and for all"],"The evidence has grown stronger, but the question is still open","'The case... looks stronger now than it did in 2018, but the debate isn't over.'",["The case for neurogenesis 'looks stronger now'.","He calls both labs 'careful'.","'The debate isn't over.'"])]))
T.append(("B06","Paleontology","""For most of the history of paleontology, the colors in dinosaur reconstructions were pure guesswork. Artists borrowed from lizards or birds and made something up. Then, around 2010, that changed, at least for a few species.

The key was a tiny structure called a melanosome. It's a capsule inside cells that holds melanin, the pigment behind black, gray, brown, and some reddish-brown colors in modern birds and mammals. Under powerful microscopes, fossil feathers turned out to contain tiny bodies with the shapes of melanosomes. And the shape matters. In living birds, long, thin, sausage-shaped melanosomes tend to go with black and gray, while rounder ones tend to go with reddish-brown. So by measuring fossil melanosomes, researchers proposed that a small feathered dinosaur from China, Sinosauropteryx, had a tail banded in reddish-brown and white.

Now, here's an irony. Those same tiny bodies had been spotted in fossils decades earlier, and researchers had interpreted them as fossilized bacteria. That wasn't unreasonable, since bacteria are about the same size and shape. A few scientists still argue that at least some of these structures are microbes. The melanosome reading has gotten stronger, though, partly because chemical traces of melanin itself have been detected along with them.

But notice what the method can't do. It only sees melanin. Many of the brightest colors in modern birds, like the reds and yellows of a cardinal or a canary, come from a different kind of pigment, called carotenoids, which don't leave these structures behind. So a dinosaur that was brilliant red could come out of this analysis looking dull brown, or blank.

So when you see a museum model with an exact color pattern, be a little suspicious. Sometimes it's evidence. Sometimes it's evidence plus a lot of hopeful filling-in.""",[
 Q("What is the talk mainly about?","medium",["How some dinosaur colors were found, and the method's limits","Why dinosaur colors will never be known with any confidence","Why most dinosaurs were probably dull brown or gray in life","Why bacteria are preserved in fossils better than feathers are"],"How some dinosaur colors were found, and the method's limits","The talk explains melanosome measurement and then 'what the method can't do'.",["Some colors were inferred 'at least for a few species'.","Dull results are an artifact of the method, not a claim about dinosaurs.","Preservation rates are never compared."]),
 Q("According to the professor, what do rounder melanosomes in living birds tend to go with?","medium",["Reddish-brown color","Black or gray color","Bright yellow color","Pure white feathers"],"Reddish-brown color","'Rounder ones tend to go with reddish-brown.'",["Long, thin melanosomes go with black and gray.","Yellows come from carotenoids, which leave no melanosomes.","White is not linked to any melanosome shape."]),
 Q("Why does the professor mention that the structures were once thought to be bacteria?","hard",["To note that the structures' nature was disputed","To show that earlier researchers had been careless","To explain how melanin was first found in fossils","To argue that the structures are certainly bacteria"],"To note that the structures' nature was disputed","'A few scientists still argue that at least some of these structures are microbes.'",["He says the reading 'wasn't unreasonable'.","Melanin traces are the newer evidence against the bacteria reading.","The melanosome reading 'has gotten stronger'."]),
 Q("What does the professor imply about a dinosaur that was bright red in life?","hard",["Melanosome analysis could miss its red color entirely","Its fossils would hold more melanin than other fossils","It would be mistaken for a fossil full of bacteria","Its color could be read from carotenoid structures"],"Melanosome analysis could miss its red color entirely","Such a dinosaur 'could come out of this analysis looking dull brown, or blank'.",["Carotenoid reds do not involve melanin.","Confusion with bacteria concerns the structures' identity, not red color.","Carotenoids 'don't leave these structures behind'."])]))
